.TH STYXREPLAY 1
.SH NAME
styxreplay \- run a program against a recorded name space
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B styxreplay
[
.B -v
]
[
.B -n
]
.I tracefile
.I cmd
.RI [ args ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Styxreplay
runs
.I cmd
with a name space that answers from a recording made by
.B iostats -t
(see
.IR iostats (4)),
in the order and at the pace it answered originally.
.PP
Because devices in Inferno are files, that covers a great deal that is
usually outside a recording: pointer and keyboard input, the draw
protocol, anything under
.BR /chan ,
and reads of
.B /dev/time
and
.BR /dev/random .
A session driven by hand can therefore be run again without the hand.
.SS Pacing
Each reply is held back for as long as it took originally, so a program
that waited two seconds for somebody to click waits two seconds again.
That is the point of recording times rather than only messages.
.PP
.B -n
answers immediately instead, which is what one wants when the question
is what the program computes rather than when.
.SS Divergence
If
.I cmd
asks for something other than what it asked for when the trace was made,
.B styxreplay
reports both requests and stops.
.PP
This is the useful output rather than an error.
The recording determines everything the program learns through its name
space, so a divergence means something outside the name space is
affecting it \(em and where it first happens is exactly what one wants
to know.
.SS What is compared
Not bytes.
Tags and fids are the client's own bookkeeping and it is free to number
them differently on a second run; the first version of this program
compared bytes and reported a divergence on the very first
.BR attach ,
where the only difference was fid 34 against fid 23.
.PP
No translation table is needed either, which is the part worth knowing:
a reply never mentions a fid.
.B Rwalk
and
.B Ropen
carry qids, which are the server's names for things and come from the
trace.
So the client may number its fids however it likes, and what is compared
is what each request actually asks for \(em the path walked, the mode
opened, the range read, the bytes written.
.SH EXAMPLE
Record a run, then reproduce it:
.IP
.EX
iostats -t /trace.styx sh /tmp/session.sh
styxreplay /trace.styx sh /tmp/session.sh
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/styxreplay.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR iostats (4),
.IR intro (5)
.SH BUGS
Two things escape a trace, so two things can make a replay diverge for
reasons that are not the program's fault.
.B Sys->millisec
and
.B Sys->sleep
are system calls rather than files, so a program that paces itself with
those is not fully recorded; one that reads
.B /dev/time
is.
And the order in which a program's own processes run is not recorded \(em
though that is a smaller gap here than elsewhere, since Dis execution is
serialised, so the schedule is a sequence of discrete handoffs rather
than an arbitrary interleaving.
.PP
A program whose behaviour depends on a reply it has not yet asked for
cannot be replayed by this at all: the recording is a sequence of
exchanges, not a model of the server.
.PP
Nothing verifies that the trace came from the same program.
Replaying one program's name space into another will simply diverge,
usually immediately, which is at least an honest way to find out.
